L tree ordered by lower bound, then upper bound, then the order","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"they were inserted in, and each vertex also records the largest upper bound in its subtree, so","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"a search skips every subtree that ends before the query begins (Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Stein, ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Introduction to Algorithms"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", §14.3). The same interval may be stored more than once,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"with different values.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["var meetings = IntervalTree<Int, String>()","meetings.insert(9 ..< 10, value: \"stand-up\")","meetings.insert(10 ..< 12, value: \"design review\")","meetings.insert(11 ..< 13, value: \"lunch\")","meetings.containing(11).map(\\.value)          \/\/ [\"design review\", \"lunch\"]","meetings.overlapping(9 ..< 11).map(\\.value)   \/\/ [\"stand-up\", \"design review\"]","meetings.contained(in: 9 ..< 12).map(\\.range) \/\/ [9 ..< 10, 10 ..< 12]"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Intervals are half-open, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"9 ..< 10"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"10 ..< 12"},{"type":"text","text":" touch without overlapping. As a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"collection, the tree’s elements are its entries in that order, read by in-order position."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"abstract":[{"text":"An interval tree: a sorted collection of half-open intervals with a value each, which finds the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"intervals overlapping a range, containing a point, or lying within a range in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"O((","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"k"}]},{"text":" + 1)